Module 2: 
Conceptual database design
End goal of this module
1.	Construct ER/ EER model (entities, attributes, keys, relationships, cardinalities) form undocumented SQL schema
2.	Identify integrity defects
i.	Missing foreign keys, orphan rows, redundant data, normalization violations
3.	Differentiate entity relationship from an attribute and surrogate key from natural Key. Plus justification of the choice
4.	Schema as a code-first data contract (Pydantic / SQLAlchemy)
5.	Requirements the schema has forgot

Story/ example of use case
Maschine in plastic injection molding company stops working and then planner asks which parts have been replaced and how often.
Main issue is that it takes a while to find the information since it is just text columns without relationship to eachother. 

Entities (thing of independent existence (a machine, a part, a technician))
Attributes (describes an entity like a machine’s serial number/ install date)
Relationships (this is the link of entities. E.g. technician doing a maintenance Job on machine)
Keys 
a.	Primary Key [PK] Unique identification of one row
b.	Foreign Key [FK] Key that lies within another table
c.	natural key (A real world identifier e.g. seriel number)
d.	surrogate key (An artificial identifier e.g. integer ID [number to uniquely identify a record in a database])
Cardinality (number of distinct values in a table column)[1to1, 1 to many, many to many]
-	{rarely used} One to one: One dataset from A ties to max one from B. Goes both ways.
-	One to many (1 to n): N means that every dataset from table A can be tied to none, one or many datasets from table B (no real limit). But data from table B can only be tied into one dataset in table A!
-	Many to many (m to n): datasets from table A can be tied to zero, one or many datasets of table B and the other way around. (sometimes you need a help table with 1: n cardinality)
Orphan row (record references to a non-existent record)

Redundant Data (storing the same information multiple times in the same database when it does not need to be duplicated. Inconsistent and storage wasting.)
Why is this not ideal/ bad?
1.	Wastes storage (same info many times)
2.	Updating data means you need to change many rows.
If not, database will be inconsistent!
3.	Insert/ delete errors 
To fix this: Store information in its own table!
Normalization Violations in databases
-	Can lead to Contradictions within the data itself
-	More complex to understand 
-	Harder to enhance and extend 
-	Vulnerable to insertion/ deletion/ update anomalies
Source for the NF rules: https://youtu.be/GFQaEYEc8_8?si=n7lhIlDXn1YBim7_


FIVE RULES of NORMALIZATION in Databases

First Normal Form Rules (1NF)
-	Using row order to convey information (violates 1NF)
-	Data types are mixed in the same column
-	No primary Key in place = violates 1NF
-	Storage of repeated group of data items in a single row  ❌1NF

Second Normal Form Rule (2NF)
-   Each non-key attribute must be dependent on primary Key

Third Normal Form Rule (3NF)
-   non key attributes must be dependent only on the Key

Fourth Normal Form Rule (4NF)
- Multi Valued dependencies are only allowed for the Key

Fifth Normal Form Rule (5NF)
-   It must not be possible to describe the table as the logical result of joining some table together



There are 5 normal forms for databases 
(Goal: making the database not express redundant information)
Levels of danger Shown in these normal forms (1 = minimum safety; 5 = Most safe)

 
